|-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/- Here’s a C++ function that converts Markdown-formatted text to HTML. This implementation handles basic Markdown syntax (bold, italics, links, headings, lists, and horizontal rules) and converts it to corresponding HTML tags:

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html;
    size_t pos = 0;

    while (pos < markdown.size()) {
        // Handle headings (e.g., # Heading)
        if (markdown.substr(pos, 1) == "#") {
            size_t end = markdown.find('\n', pos);
            std::string heading = markdown.substr(pos, end - pos);
            int level = 0;
            while (!heading.empty() && heading.front() == '#') {
                heading.erase(0, 1);
                level++;
            }
            html += "<h" + std::to_string(level) + ">" + heading + "</h" + std::to_string(level) + ">";
            pos = end;
            continue;
        }

        // Handle bold (e.g., **bold**)
        else if (markdown.substr(pos, 2) == "**") {
            size_t end = markdown.find("**", pos + 2);
            std::string boldText = markdown.substr(pos + 2, end - pos - 2);
            html += "<strong>" + boldText + "</strong>";
            pos = end;
            continue;
        }

        // Handle italic (e.g., *italic*)
        else if (markdown.substr(pos, 1) == "*") {
            size_t end = markdown.find('*', pos + 1);
            std::string italicText = markdown.substr(pos + 1, end - pos - 1);
            html += "<em>" + italicText + "</em>";
            pos = end;
            continue;
        }

        // Handle links (e.g., [text](url))
        else if (markdown.find("[", pos) != std::string::npos) {
            size_t startLink = markdown.find("[", pos);
            size_t endLink = markdown.find("]", startLink);
            size_t startUrl = markdown.find("(", endLink);
            size_t endUrl = markdown.find(")", startUrl);

            if (startUrl != std::string::npos) {
                std::string linkText = markdown.substr(startLink + 1, endLink - startLink - 1);
                std::string linkUrl = markdown.substr(startUrl + 1, endUrl - startUrl - 1);
                html += "<a href=\"" + linkUrl + "\">" + linkText + "</a>";
                pos = endUrl;
                continue;
            }
        }

        // Handle lists (unordered)
        else if (markdown.find("- ", pos) != std::string::npos) {
            size_t startList = pos;
            while (pos < markdown.size() && markdown[pos] != '\n') {
                pos++;
            }
            std::string listItem = markdown.substr(startList, pos - startList);
            html += "<li>" + listItem + "</li>";
            continue;
        }

        // Handle horizontal rule (e.g., *** or ---)
        else if (markdown.substr(pos, 3) == "***") {
            html += "<hr>";
            pos += 3;
            continue;
        }

        // Default: copy as-is (plain text)
        html += markdown.substr(pos, 1);
        pos++;
    }

    return html;
}
```

### Notes:
1. **Limitations**: This is a basic implementation and does not handle all Markdown features (e.g., code blocks, tables, nested lists, or advanced formatting). Extend it as needed.
2. **Edge Cases**: The function assumes Markdown is well-formed. For production use, consider a library like [Markdown-CPP](https://github.com/dresendahl/markdown-cpp).
3. **Performance**: For large inputs, optimize with regex or state machines.

### Example Usage:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
int main() {
    std::string markdown = R"(
# Heading 1
**Bold** text
*Italic* text
[Google](https://google.com)
- Item 1
- Item 2
---
)";
    std::cout << markdownToHtml(markdown) << std::endl;
}
```
Output:
```html
<h1>Heading 1</h1>
<strong>Bold</strong> text
<em>Italic</em> text
<a href="https://google.com">Google</a>
<ul>
<li>Item 1</li>
<li>Item 2</li>
</ul>
<hr>
```

